{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfEBEERLx3AhV8WnazZ6o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015Ry5829PeG28VoPdxFmh6C","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/thomas/.local/share/hivedispatch/repos/thomasmeadows__HiveDispatch/SCRUM-5/docs/setup.md","offset":125,"limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6704,"cache_read_input_tokens":48287,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6704},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2daaed25-284e-4780-838f-5b169e1711d5","uuid":"bb49f7e6-6ee9-4734-9886-5a3a4d8b4e33","timestamp":"2026-09-20T05:57:19.690Z","request_id":"req_011CfEBEDt6UPPgmQGaXJJHZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015Ry5829PeG28VoPdxFmh6C":{"file_path":"/home/thomas/.local/share/hivedispatch/repos/thomasmeadows__HiveDispatch/SCRUM-5/docs/setup.md","offset":125,"limit":50}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Ry5829PeG28VoPdxFmh6C","type":"tool_result","content":"125\tThe worker runs with your git credentials and can push any branch your credentials allow. Scope the deploy key or token to branch creation and PR opening where your host supports it, never to the default branch, and run the worker under a dedicated account when you can.\n126\t\n127\t## Verify\n128\t\n129\t```sh\n130\thivedispatch check -jira\n131\thivedispatch run -once -placeholder   # takes one Ready ticket to a PR with a placeholder commit\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\t## 8. GitHub Issues instead of Jira\n135\t\n136\tSet `tracker: github` in the worker config and skip sections 1–4. The GitHub token (section 5) then also needs **Issues: read and write**. State lives in labels and the claim in a hidden marker in the issue body, so nothing else has to exist in the repository.\n137\t\n138\t```sh\n139\thivedispatch init -github     # creates hive:ready, hive:in-progress, hive:needs-info, hive:in-review, hive:needs-human in each repo\n140\thivedispatch check -live\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\tTo queue an issue, add the **`hive:ready`** label (one tap in the GitHub mobile app). The worker swaps the label as the ticket moves: `hive:in-progress` while it works, `hive:needs-info` when it has a question — answer in the thread and put `hive:ready` back — `hive:in-review` when a pull request is open, `hive:needs-human` when it will not attempt the issue. Ticket keys are `<project>-<issue number>` with `project` from `repos[].project`, so issue #12 in a repo with `project: HD` is `HD-12` and its branch is `hive/HD-12`.\n144\t\n145\tLabel names are configurable under `github.labels`.\n146\t\n147\tChoose a `project` that no Jira project on the same worker uses. Ticket keys are the identity for branches, run records and worktrees; `SCRUM-5` from Jira and issue #5 in a repo with `project: SCRUM` would share all three.\n148\t\n149\t## 9. Claude Code\n150\t\n151\tThe worker shells out to the `claude` CLI. Log in once as the user that runs the worker (`claude` then `/login`) — headless runs reuse the stored credentials. Do not set `--bare` anywhere; it skips credential loading.\n152\t\n153\tPer-repo policy lives in `.hivedispatch.yaml` at the repository root:\n154\t\n155\t```yaml\n156\texecutor:\n157\t  model: sonnet                 # optional; default is the CLI's default\n158\t  permission_mode: dontAsk      # acceptEdits | auto | bypassPermissions | manual | dontAsk | plan\n159\t  tools: [default]              # built-in tool set; use a list to restrict\n160\t  allowed_tools:                # pre-approved patterns for dontAsk mode\n161\t    - Edit\n162\t    - \"Bash(go test:*)\"\n163\t  max_budget_usd: 5             # optional; API-billing accounts only\n164\tguidance: |\n165\t  Free text appended to every prompt for this repo.\n166\t```\n167\t\n168\t`dontAsk` fails closed: anything not in `allowed_tools` is denied and the agent must work around it or ask. `bypassPermissions` is for sandboxed runs only.\n169\t\n170\tAllowlist patterns match whole tokens by prefix, so `Bash(golangci-lint:*)` allows `golangci-lint run ./...` but not `/home/me/go/bin/golangci-lint run` — put tool directories on the agent's `PATH` with `executor.path` instead of allowing absolute paths.\n171\t\n172\tThe run log for each attempt is saved to the state branch under `logs/<KEY>/<timestamp>.log`.\n173\t\n174\t### Triage"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2daaed25-284e-4780-838f-5b169e1711d5","uuid":"78ffce60-b15b-4c8e-bece-d8d066b457a5","timestamp":"2026-09-20T05:57:19.695Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/thomas/.local/share/hivedispatch/repos/thomasmeadows__HiveDispatch/SCRUM-5/docs/setup.md","content":"The worker runs with your git credentials and can push any branch your credentials allow. Scope the deploy key or token to branch creation and PR opening where your host supports it, never to the default branch, and run the worker under a dedicated account when you can.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nhivedispatch check -jira\nhivedispatch run -once -placeholder   # takes one Ready ticket to a PR with a placeholder commit\n```\n\n## 8. GitHub Issues instead of Jira\n\nSet `tracker: github` in the worker config and skip sections 1–4. The GitHub token (section 5) then also needs **Issues: read and write**. State lives in labels and the claim in a hidden marker in the issue body, so nothing else has to exist in the repository.\n\n```sh\nhivedispatch init -github     # creates hive:ready, hive:in-progress, hive:needs-info, hive:in-review, hive:needs-human in each repo\nhivedispatch check -live\n```\n\nTo queue an issue, add the **`hive:ready`** label (one tap in the GitHub mobile app). The worker swaps the label as the ticket moves: `hive:in-progress` while it works, `hive:needs-info` when it has a question — answer in the thread and put `hive:ready` back — `hive:in-review` when a pull request is open, `hive:needs-human` when it will not attempt the issue. Ticket keys are `<project>-<issue number>` with `project` from `repos[].project`, so issue #12 in a repo with `project: HD` is `HD-12` and its branch is `hive/HD-12`.\n\nLabel names are configurable under `github.labels`.\n\nChoose a `project` that no Jira project on the same worker uses. Ticket keys are the identity for branches, run records and worktrees; `SCRUM-5` from Jira and issue #5 in a repo with `project: SCRUM` would share all three.\n\n## 9. Claude Code\n\nThe worker shells out to the `claude` CLI. Log in once as the user that runs the worker (`claude` then `/login`) — headless runs reuse the stored credentials. Do not set `--bare` anywhere; it skips credential loading.\n\nPer-repo policy lives in `.hivedispatch.yaml` at the repository root:\n\n```yaml\nexecutor:\n  model: sonnet                 # optional; default is the CLI's default\n  permission_mode: dontAsk      # acceptEdits | auto | bypassPermissions | manual | dontAsk | plan\n  tools: [default]              # built-in tool set; use a list to restrict\n  allowed_tools:                # pre-approved patterns for dontAsk mode\n    - Edit\n    - \"Bash(go test:*)\"\n  max_budget_usd: 5             # optional; API-billing accounts only\nguidance: |\n  Free text appended to every prompt for this repo.\n```\n\n`dontAsk` fails closed: anything not in `allowed_tools` is denied and the agent must work around it or ask. `bypassPermissions` is for sandboxed runs only.\n\nAllowlist patterns match whole tokens by prefix, so `Bash(golangci-lint:*)` allows `golangci-lint run ./...` but not `/home/me/go/bin/golangci-lint run` — put tool directories on the agent's `PATH` with `executor.path` instead of allowing absolute paths.\n\nThe run log for each attempt is saved to the state branch under `logs/<KEY>/<timestamp>.log`.\n\n### Triage","numLines":50,"startLine":125,"totalLines":185}}}
